   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( y = h(x) \\) be a solution of the differential equation \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = x^{3} + 2 x^{2} - 15 x \\), defined for \\( -1 < x < 4 \\). Find the value of \\( x \\) at which \\( h \\) has a relative minimum."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 3
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The differential equation gives the derivative directly: \\( h'(x) = x^{3} + 2 x^{2} - 15 x \\) for \\( -1 < x < 4 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            "x",
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               -3
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               5
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Set \\( h'(x) = 0 \\) and factor: \\( x(x + 5)(x - 3) = 0 \\), so \\( x = -5, 0, 3 \\).",
         "mathjson": 3
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Only \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 3 \\) lie in \\( -1 < x < 4 \\); \\( x = -5 \\) is outside the interval on which \\( h \\) is defined, so it is not a critical point of \\( h \\).",
         "mathjson": 3
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "\\( h' \\) changes from negative to positive at \\( x = 3 \\) and from positive to negative at \\( x = 0 \\), so \\( h \\) has its relative minimum at \\( x = 3 \\).",
         "mathjson": 3
      }
   ],
